WEBVTT

00:00:00.251 --> 00:00:04.849
Mia, an ice cube melts, a sheet of
paper is cut up, and a log burns.

00:00:05.365 --> 00:00:07.959
Which change definitely
makes new substances?

00:00:08.751 --> 00:00:12.966
All of them look different, so all
three must be chemical reactions.

00:00:13.658 --> 00:00:15.035
Appearance is not enough.

00:00:15.376 --> 00:00:19.076
Melting changes water's state,
and cutting changes paper's shape.

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:21.266
Neither alone makes a new substance.

00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:24.793
Burning wood produces new
substances, including gases

00:00:24.794 --> 00:00:26.595
and ash, so it is chemical.

00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:32.134
Physical means the substance stays the
same even if its shape or state changes.

00:00:32.698 --> 00:00:35.065
Chemical means new substances form.

00:00:35.839 --> 00:00:36.685
Exactly.

00:00:37.081 --> 00:00:40.421
Dissolving sugar in water is
another familiar physical change.

00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:43.667
The sugar spreads through the
water rather than being destroyed.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:46.291
We can recover it by
appropriate separation.

00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:51.360
If I cannot easily reverse something,
does that automatically make it chemical?

00:00:51.946 --> 00:00:54.531
Reassembling shredded
paper would take forever.

00:00:55.361 --> 00:00:56.538
Excellent challenge.

00:00:57.019 --> 00:01:00.147
Ease of reversal is only a
clue, not the definition.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.254
Some physical changes are
difficult to undo, and some

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:04.977
chemical reactions are reversible.

00:01:05.419 --> 00:01:07.813
Ask whether the substances
have changed chemically.

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:12.390
That rescues me from writing hard
to reverse as my whole explanation.

00:01:13.145 --> 00:01:16.937
Reactions can produce color
changes, gas, temperature

00:01:16.937 --> 00:01:19.500
changes, light, or a precipitate.

00:01:19.966 --> 00:01:24.283
A precipitate is an insoluble solid
formed from a reaction in solution.

00:01:24.931 --> 00:01:27.916
Cloudiness may show tiny
solid particles appearing.

00:01:28.698 --> 00:01:32.732
But boiling also makes bubbles, and
adding food coloring changes color.

00:01:33.113 --> 00:01:34.475
Neither proves a reaction.

00:01:35.205 --> 00:01:35.777
Correct.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.116
Even several dramatic
observations need interpretation.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:42.735
We collect evidence for new
substances rather than awarding

00:01:42.738 --> 00:01:46.232
chemical reaction status to anything
that fizzes or surprises us.

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:49.667
Science detective, not
special effects judge.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:51.092
That is my first rule

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:54.837
The substances present at the
start of a reaction are reactants.

00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:57.410
The substances formed are products.

00:01:57.736 --> 00:02:01.475
On our board, reactants go on
the left, an arrow points right,

00:02:01.711 --> 00:02:03.176
and products go on the right.

00:02:03.977 --> 00:02:07.886
The arrow means reacts to form,
not that every object on the

00:02:07.886 --> 00:02:09.408
two sides looks identical.

00:02:10.077 --> 00:02:10.516
Right.

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:16.419
A word equation might say magnesium
plus oxygen forms magnesium oxide.

00:02:16.882 --> 00:02:21.542
A symbol equation uses chemical formulae,
letting us count atoms precisely.

00:02:22.287 --> 00:02:28.070
Oxygen is O2, magnesium is Mg,
and magnesium oxide is MgO.

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:30.880
Why does the equation need big numbers?

00:02:31.655 --> 00:02:36.353
Because oxygen arrives in pairs of atoms,
and we must account for every atom.

00:02:37.171 --> 00:02:41.794
Two magnesium atoms react with one
oxygen molecule to give magnesium

00:02:41.812 --> 00:02:46.020
oxide in a two-to-two ratio
of magnesium and oxygen atoms.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:51.391
We write two Mg plus O2 arrow two MgO.

00:02:51.917 --> 00:02:54.097
Same atom counts before and after.

00:02:54.752 --> 00:02:55.529
Exactly.

00:02:55.804 --> 00:02:58.101
The coefficient is the
big number in front.

00:02:58.317 --> 00:03:00.433
It multiplies the entire formula.

00:03:00.864 --> 00:03:04.159
A subscript is the small number
attached to an element symbol.

00:03:04.566 --> 00:03:09.989
Two H2O represents four hydrogen
atoms and two oxygen atoms altogether.

00:03:10.818 --> 00:03:14.044
I used to read it as two
hydrogens and two oxygens.

00:03:14.481 --> 00:03:17.033
The coefficient has more
work to do than I thought.

00:03:17.715 --> 00:03:18.306
It does.

00:03:18.658 --> 00:03:20.672
Read the whole formula before counting.

00:03:21.105 --> 00:03:22.838
A missing subscript means one.

00:03:23.316 --> 00:03:28.365
Also remember that some solids, such as
magnesium oxide, have repeating structures

00:03:28.366 --> 00:03:30.161
rather than separate little molecules.

00:03:30.908 --> 00:03:33.136
So the particle drawing
is a counting model.

00:03:33.507 --> 00:03:37.397
It does not mean every substance
consists of tiny detachable

00:03:37.409 --> 00:03:38.920
formula-shaped clusters.

00:03:39.325 --> 00:03:42.293
Imagine tracking every atom
involved in a reaction.

00:03:42.937 --> 00:03:45.790
Are new atoms created when
a new substance forms?

00:03:46.451 --> 00:03:48.395
New substance, new atoms?

00:03:48.831 --> 00:03:50.187
That sounds like another trap.

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:51.512
It is.

00:03:51.825 --> 00:03:54.809
Ordinary chemical
reactions rearrange atoms.

00:03:55.094 --> 00:03:57.030
They do not create or destroy them.

00:03:57.398 --> 00:04:00.877
If no matter enters or leaves
the system, the total mass of

00:04:00.878 --> 00:04:03.026
reactants and products is conserved.

00:04:03.742 --> 00:04:08.383
Same ingredients at atom level,
different arrangements, like moving the

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:10.496
same building blocks into a new model.

00:04:11.316 --> 00:04:15.547
Then explain this: magnesium burned
in air can leave a heavier oxide.

00:04:15.912 --> 00:04:17.548
Has conservation of mass failed?

00:04:18.326 --> 00:04:20.760
Oxygen from the air has
joined the magnesium.

00:04:21.382 --> 00:04:24.899
If we weighed only the original
magnesium, we missed the oxygen

00:04:24.907 --> 00:04:26.412
entering our chosen system.

00:04:27.109 --> 00:04:27.895
Exactly.

00:04:28.152 --> 00:04:32.100
The product's extra mass came from
outside that initial weighed material.

00:04:32.527 --> 00:04:36.307
Counting the magnesium and the oxygen
that reacted restores the balance.

00:04:37.097 --> 00:04:40.080
A reaction can also appear to lose mass
if a gas escapes from an open container.
